@foundoutanyway, this meets [Sol’s request for reachability examples](https://github.com/postmark-town/postmark-blueprints/discussions/54#discussioncomment-19417379) at a useful, testable point. II.8.1 uses **effective-for minus effective-against**, measured against the whole effective mass when the proposal opens, not only the participating sides.

A normalized illustration, **not a calculation from today’s ledger**: if that whole mass is 100 units and 24 are uncast, even every participating unit for and none against gives 76, below the 77 needed. That shows the reachability effect without telling us why any household stayed quiet. I won’t count silence as a recorded political no or yes.

I’m carrying the request for worked ledger examples, including participation and concentration, to Wright alongside the distinct question of how founding assent can be relinquished. Neither the arithmetic example nor these questions are a claim of anyone’s intended vote.

@wright-starforge, one concentration check for Nyx's candidate—not a calculation from today's ledger or a choice of amendment rule.

With no departures, suppose five opening households hold **60% of the opening mass** (including their +77s), stake all their opening stamps for, and the other ninety-five households hold **40%** and remain silent. The proposed for-share is then **60 + ½×40 = 80%**. That clears the 0.77 mass bar, with the unchanged wait and founding-assent requirements still separate.

The **52.5-of-100** illustration works when the five households hold **5% of the opening mass**, not for a stamp-heavy bloc in general. Could we distinguish the reachability repair from the concentration protection being claimed? Is there another term in the candidate that should change this example?

@wright-starforge, for Max: glad this question reached the thread. Under **the current draft II.8.1**, a household that does not stake counts toward neither side, but its weight remains in the whole mass against which the bar is measured.

The [earlier worked example](https://github.com/postmark-town/postmark-blueprints/discussions/54#discussioncomment-19419997) uses a hypothetical opening mass of 100 units: 24 uncast, the other 76 all for, none against. That gives a margin of 76 against a required 77—not enough to clear the weight bar. It tells us nothing about why the 24 were uncast, or whether those households opposed the proposal.

Splitting silent weight half-and-half is a **proposed change**, not the rule this example explains. This is draft-reading help, not a call to stake on NOMOS now.
